class_name LegendStory
extends RefCounted

## Each legend's story, told in its fight's cut scenes (LegendCutscene): the weather it brings, the
## introduction as it enters, then a chapter more as it drops to 66% HP (it calls for help) and to
## 33% (it enrages). Drawn from the folklore of each legend's real hometown.

## weather: storm (rain and lightning), fog, snow, swamp (mist and fireflies), dust (blowing grit),
## embers (rising sparks), sky (streaks falling from the stars).
const STORIES := {
	"mothman": {"weather": "storm",
		"intro": "Point Pleasant, 1966. Two couples drove past the old TNT plant and saw it: a man-shaped thing with great wings and eyes like red reflectors.",
		"chapters": ["For a year it was seen over the river towns. Some said it came to warn, not to harm.",
			"Then the Silver Bridge fell into the Ohio. People still say the Mothman knew it was coming."]},
	"woodbooger": {"weather": "fog",
		"intro": "Deep in the hollows of Wise County, hunters tell of a shaggy giant that steps between the trees without a sound.",
		"chapters": ["Norton put up a statue of it at Flag Rock. The woods put up nothing. They just keep it.",
			"Those who've heard its howl say the whole ridge goes quiet after. Tonight the ridge is very quiet."]},
	"flatwoods_monster": {"weather": "sky",
		"intro": "September 1952. A light fell out of the sky over Braxton County, and a group of kids climbed the hill to find it.",
		"chapters": ["At the top: a ten-foot figure in a spade-shaped hood, its face glowing, a sour mist rolling off it.",
			"They ran, and they were sick for days. Whatever it was, it never left the hill."]},
	"wampus_cat": {"weather": "fog",
		"intro": "In the Smokies they say a woman once hid in a cougar's hide to spy on the hunters' secret rites.",
		"chapters": ["She was caught, and cursed to wear the hide forever: half woman, half cat, always on the prowl.",
			"Her yowl carries for miles on still nights. Hear it, and she already knows where you are."]},
	"pope_lick_monster": {"weather": "storm",
		"intro": "A railroad trestle towers over Pope Lick Creek. Under it, folks say, lives something with a man's body and a goat's legs.",
		"chapters": ["It calls in a voice that sounds like your own thoughts, luring the curious out onto the tracks.",
			"The trains still run. And the thing under the trestle still waits."]},
	"loveland_frogman": {"weather": "storm",
		"intro": "Loveland, Ohio, 1972. On a wet night a police officer saw something crouched in the road. Then it stood up.",
		"chapters": ["Four feet tall, leathery, with a frog's face. It hopped the guardrail into the Little Miami River.",
			"Old tales say it carries a wand that throws sparks. Tonight it brought its temper instead."]},
	"jersey_devil": {"weather": "storm",
		"intro": "1735. In the Pine Barrens, Mother Leeds cursed her thirteenth child before it was born.",
		"chapters": ["It came out a horse-headed, bat-winged thing, and flew up the chimney into the storm.",
			"In 1909 its hoofprints crossed rooftops all over South Jersey. Schools closed. Mills shut. It never left."]},
	"goatman": {"weather": "fog",
		"intro": "Fletchertown Road, Maryland. Teenagers parked out there tell of a figure with horns and an axe.",
		"chapters": ["Some say it was a scientist whose experiment went wrong at the farm labs nearby.",
			"Others just say: don't stop the car. Too late for that now."]},
	"snallygaster": {"weather": "storm",
		"intro": "German settlers in the Maryland hills told of the Schneller Geist, the quick spirit: half bird, half dragon.",
		"chapters": ["They painted seven-pointed stars on their barns to keep it away. Some barns still have them.",
			"In 1932 the papers swore it drowned in a vat of moonshine. The papers were wrong."]},
	"champ": {"weather": "fog",
		"intro": "Lake Champlain runs deep and cold between Vermont and New York. Something long has lived in it for centuries.",
		"chapters": ["In 1977 a family on the shore photographed a neck and head rising from the water.",
			"Hundreds have seen it since. Tonight it came out of the water to meet you."]},
	"dover_demon": {"weather": "fog",
		"intro": "Dover, Massachusetts, April 1977. A teenager driving home caught it in his headlights, crouched on a stone wall.",
		"chapters": ["A huge melon head, glowing orange eyes, limbs like wet sticks. Three people saw it in two nights.",
			"Then it was gone, as if it had never been. Until now."]},
	"dogman": {"weather": "storm",
		"intro": "Northern Michigan. In years ending in seven, they say, the Dogman walks.",
		"chapters": ["Seven feet tall, a man's body, a dog's head, and a howl that sounds like a scream.",
			"A DJ wrote a song about it in 1987 as a joke. Then the phone calls started."]},
	"beast_of_bray_road": {"weather": "fog",
		"intro": "Bray Road, outside Elkhorn, Wisconsin. Late-night drivers saw something kneeling in the ditch.",
		"chapters": ["It held its meal in its paws like hands. Then it stood up on two legs.",
			"A local reporter collected dozens of sightings. Most of them end the same way: it ran at the car."]},
	"hodag": {"weather": "embers",
		"intro": "Rhinelander, Wisconsin, 1893. A timber man named Shepard swore he'd found a horned, spiked beast that stank of buzzard and skunk.",
		"chapters": ["He said he killed it with dynamite. Three years later he caught another and showed it at the fair.",
			"People called it a hoax: a man with wires behind a curtain. This one isn't on wires."]},
	"van_meter_visitor": {"weather": "storm",
		"intro": "Van Meter, Iowa, 1903. A creature with bat wings and a glowing horn lit up the town at night.",
		"chapters": ["The townsfolk shot at it. The bullets did nothing. They tracked it to an old coal mine.",
			"It came out with a second one, screamed, and flew off. The mine was sealed. Something opened it again."]},
	"ozark_howler": {"weather": "storm",
		"intro": "In the Ozark hills, hunters hear a howl that's half wolf, half elk, and all wrong.",
		"chapters": ["Those who see it describe a bear-sized black cat with horns and eyes that burn.",
			"Some say just seeing it means bad luck. You've seen plenty of it now."]},
	"fouke_monster": {"weather": "swamp",
		"intro": "Fouke, Arkansas, 1971. Something tall, hairy and stinking reached through the Ford family's window one night.",
		"chapters": ["Its tracks had three toes. Its trail led back into Boggy Creek.",
			"They made a movie about it the next year. It never got a cut of the money. It's still sore about that."]},
	"rougarou": {"weather": "swamp",
		"intro": "In the bayous of south Louisiana, grandmothers warn: break your Lent, and the Rougarou will come for you.",
		"chapters": ["A man with a wolf's head, it hunts by moonlight through the cypress and the moss.",
			"The curse passes on when it draws blood. It's been trying for a long, long time."]},
	"lizard_man": {"weather": "swamp",
		"intro": "Bishopville, South Carolina, 1988. A teenager changing a flat at 2 a.m. by Scape Ore Swamp heard something behind him.",
		"chapters": ["Seven feet, green and scaled, three fingers, red eyes. It leapt onto his roof as he drove off.",
			"The sheriff found clawed-up cars and huge tracks. The swamp kept the rest."]},
	"altamaha_ha": {"weather": "fog",
		"intro": "The Altamaha River winds slow and dark to the Georgia coast. The fishermen there all know of Altie.",
		"chapters": ["Long as a boat, with a crocodile's snout and flippers that slap the water.",
			"People told of it long before the bridges and the boats. It was here first, and it wants it back."]},
	"skunk_ape": {"weather": "swamp",
		"intro": "The Florida Everglades, deep in the sawgrass. You smell it long before you see it.",
		"chapters": ["A seven-foot ape that reeks of rotten eggs and swamp gas, hiding out in old alligator dens.",
			"In 2000 someone photographed one in their yard, stealing apples. Tonight it wants more than apples."]},
	"chupacabra": {"weather": "dust",
		"intro": "Texas ranch country. Ranchers wake to find their goats drained of blood, with two neat holes in the neck.",
		"chapters": ["It was first seen in Puerto Rico in 1995: spines down its back, eyes that glow red.",
			"In Texas it went gray and hairless, and it kept hunting. It's thirsty again."]},
	"mogollon_monster": {"weather": "dust",
		"intro": "The Mogollon Rim cuts across Arizona like a wall. Campers up top hear whistling at night, and stones thrown from the dark.",
		"chapters": ["Seven feet of reddish-black hair, a smell of dead fish and skunk, a scream that freezes the blood.",
			"Hikers come back to camps torn apart. It doesn't like visitors on its Rim."]},
	"bigfoot": {"weather": "storm",
		"intro": "The Pacific Northwest. Rain, moss, and trees as tall as towers. Somewhere among them, a giant walks.",
		"chapters": ["In 1958 loggers at Bluff Creek found footprints sixteen inches long. They called it Bigfoot.",
			"In 1967 two riders filmed it walking away, looking back over its shoulder. It isn't walking away now."]},
	"tahoe_tessie": {"weather": "fog",
		"intro": "Lake Tahoe is so deep and cold that, they say, what sinks in it never comes back up.",
		"chapters": ["Divers and boaters tell of something huge moving beneath them: Tessie, long and dark.",
			"They say one diver saw her down there and never spoke of it again. Now you know why."]},
	"bear_lake_monster": {"weather": "fog",
		"intro": "Bear Lake, on the Utah-Idaho line. In 1868 a newspaper told of a serpent with legs that swam faster than a horse could run.",
		"chapters": ["Decades later the writer admitted it was a whopper. Nobody told the serpent.",
			"The lake turns turquoise in summer. Tonight it's red with fury."]},
	"flathead_monster": {"weather": "fog",
		"intro": "Flathead Lake, Montana. Since 1889 more than a hundred people have reported something big in the water.",
		"chapters": ["Long and dark, with a shiny back and a wake like a boat, but there's never a boat.",
			"Just a big fish story, say the skeptics. This fish has teeth."]},
	"slide_rock_bolter": {"weather": "snow",
		"intro": "High in the Colorado Rockies, old lumberjacks warned of a beast as big as a whale, clinging to the peaks by its hooked tail.",
		"chapters": ["When it spots someone below, it lets go and slides down the mountain, mouth wide open.",
			"A ranger once tricked one into swallowing a pile of dynamite. Its kin never forgot."]},
	"iliamna_monster": {"weather": "snow",
		"intro": "Lake Iliamna, Alaska. Cold, huge and remote: the only way in is by plane.",
		"chapters": ["Bush pilots flying low have seen shapes in the water as long as their planes.",
			"The locals say it can bite a boat in half. You're not in a boat."]},
	"black_shuck": {"weather": "storm",
		"intro": "East Anglia, on the English coast. On stormy nights a black dog as big as a calf walks the lanes, its eyes burning.",
		"chapters": ["In 1577 it burst into Bungay church in a thunderstorm. Scorch marks are still on the door at Blythburgh.",
			"They say to see it is to die within the year. Better make this year count."]},
	"mokele_mbembe": {"weather": "swamp",
		"intro": "The Congo basin. Vast rivers, endless forest, and something the people there say holds back the water.",
		"chapters": ["Its name means 'one who stops the flow of rivers': big as an elephant, with a long neck and tail.",
			"Expeditions have searched for decades and found only tracks. It found you first."]},
	"yeti": {"weather": "snow",
		"intro": "The high Himalaya, where the air is thin and the snow never melts.",
		"chapters": ["In 1951 climbers photographed a footprint far bigger than a boot. The Sherpa had a name for it long before.",
			"The Abominable Snowman, the papers called it. It has heard enough of that name."]},
	"drop_bear": {"weather": "dust",
		"intro": "Australia, under the gum trees. The locals tell visitors to keep an eye on the branches overhead.",
		"chapters": ["A meat-eating cousin of the koala, they say, that drops from the trees onto its prey's head.",
			"Vegemite behind the ears keeps them off, supposedly. Your pack forgot the Vegemite."]},
}


## `legend_id`'s story, or a plain one for a legend with none written yet.
static func of(legend_id: String) -> Dictionary:
	if STORIES.has(legend_id):
		return STORIES[legend_id]
	var lore := String(GameData.LEGENDS.get(legend_id, {}).get("lore", "Something old stirs."))
	return {"weather": "storm", "intro": lore, "chapters": ["It has been waiting a long time for this.", "Now it's angry."]}
